sweet ufs: keep the kick line alive without kernel timeouts

bp15's ring froze at the same second for all three threads the driver
owns, while the console kept printing userland output for another six
seconds: the heartbeat and the flusher sleep on tsleep (), and the kick
thread did too, so they stop together the moment the kernel stops
delivering timeouts - which is what every "console stopped" boot has
looked like, bp11 included (its flusher froze at beats 2 with no command
in flight).

Make the kick thread spin with IODelay () instead, so it needs no
timeouts, and print mach_absolute_time () in its line: a spin that keeps
appending proves the machine is alive, and whether the printed time
advances separates a dead clock from a dead wakeup path.
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
@@ -1276,21 +1276,29 @@
 {
 	uint32_t n = 0;
 
+	extern void IODelay(unsigned int);
+
 	for (;;) {
-		char line[192];
+		char line[208];
 		uint64_t ns = 0, age = 0, last;
 		int len;
 
-		(void)tsleep(&sweet_hb_chan, PZERO, "sweetkick", hz * 2);
+		/* Busy wait, not tsleep (): bp15 showed this thread, the heartbeat
+		 * and the flusher all stop at the same second while the console
+		 * keeps printing, i.e. every thread sleeping on a timeout freezes
+		 * together.  A spin needs no timeouts, and reading the clock here
+		 * says whether time itself stopped. */
+		IODelay(2000000);
 		last = sweet_log_last_g;
 		if (last != 0 && mach_absolute_time() > last) {
 			absolutetime_to_nanoseconds(mach_absolute_time() - last, &ns);
 			age = ns / 1000000ull;
 		}
 		len = snprintf(line, sizeof(line),
-		    "SWEET KICK %u: log %u seq %u beats %u fails %u ferr %d fage %llu"
+		    "SWEET KICK %u: t %llu log %u seq %u beats %u fails %u ferr %d fage %llu"
 		    " | cmd %u err %d x%u lk %u/%u st %u pp %u/%u\n",
-		    ++n, (unsigned)os_atomic_load(&sweet_log_total, relaxed),
+		    ++n, (unsigned long long)(mach_absolute_time() % 1000000000ull),
+		    (unsigned)os_atomic_load(&sweet_log_total, relaxed),
 		    sweet_log_seq_g, (unsigned)sweet_log_beats, sweet_log_fails_g,
 		    sweet_log_ferr_g, (unsigned long long)age,
 		    (unsigned)su.cmd_serial, su.last_err, (unsigned)su.err_serial,
